\section{Use One Instruction Representation at the Center}

A register name in a text file, a five-bit field in an instruction, and an operand of an instruction in a compiler are different representations of the same architectural choice. An assembler validates text and emits bytes, a decoder identifies an instruction and extracts its fields from bytes, and a disassembler then prints those decoded operands. In the February 2024 VADL preprint, arXiv:2402.09087v1, Section~4.5 describes these paths in the LLVM Compiler Backend (LCB) prototype \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. One internal instruction may be constructed by the compiler backend or by the assembler parser; parsing is unnecessary when the compiler calls the emitter directly.

The following VADL clauses are the ADDI instance obtained by substituting arguments into the model in Listing~3. They use its \texttt{Itype} layout and register file, and are not a complete specification.

\begin{verbatim}
encoding ADDI = { opcode = 0b001'0011, funct3 = 0b000 }
assembly ADDI = (mnemonic, " ", register(rd), ", ",
                 register(rs1), ", ", decimal(imm))
\end{verbatim}

The two clauses supply different evidence. \texttt{encoding} tells the binary tools which fields are fixed for this instruction. \texttt{assembly} tells the printer which pieces of the internal instruction contribute to its textual form. Neither describes the arithmetic performed when ADDI executes. \Cref{fig:visual-327} puts the parser, emitter, decoder, and printer on their respective edges; link-time fixups are a separate part of the object path.

