\section{Generate the Table, Then Choose the Search}

Section~4.5.3 explains that LLVM can synthesize a decoder from the format and constant fields expressed in TableGen. Section~4.5.4 uses the same infrastructure for machine-code emission. Section~4.6 describes a different consumer of decoding information: the functional simulator reuses its decoder from VIR. A generator does not have to make all targets use the same runtime algorithm; it has to make them agree on which words and operands those algorithms recognize.

For fixed-width instructions, a simple reference implementation tests a word against one mask and fixed value per instruction, then applies any additional field constraints. An optimized one might first dispatch on the primary opcode, then inspect the function bits only for encodings in that group. A mask match alone may not prove that a candidate satisfies its constraints. If two candidates accept the same word and the architecture does not define an alias policy, optimization must not introduce a winner by accidentally reordering the tests. Variable-length instructions introduce another question: can the decoder determine which additional bytes to read, and can it report an incomplete instruction without reading them?

Record the number of bytes consumed as part of a decode test. A disassembler that identifies the right first mnemonic but advances by the wrong length will decode every subsequent instruction from the wrong boundary. On a variable-length target, a short instruction followed by a longer one makes a better basic test than two instructions that happen to be the same size.

