\section{Walk Bytes in Both Directions}

This independent teaching calculation uses the Listing~3 fields and a simple immediate to avoid ambiguities about spelling signed constants.

\begin{verbatim}
input: addi x5, x6, 7
  parse -> ADDI(rd=5, rs1=6, imm=7)  # validate operands first
  encode: (7<<20) | (6<<15) | (5<<7) | 0x13
  word = 0x00730293
  emit little-endian: 93 02 73 00

decode 93 02 73 00:
  word & 0x0000707f = 0x00000013   # opcode and funct3
  extract rd=5, rs1=6, imm=7
  print canonical ADDI spelling
\end{verbatim}

If \texttt{funct3} becomes binary 110 while all other fields stay fixed, the word becomes \texttt{0x00736293}; Listing~3 assigns that pattern to ORI. This paired test actually detects a decoder that ignores the function bits. Retain it when changing the generated decision structure. Also supply unsupported words, register aliases, and boundary immediates. The useful identities are decode-after-encode on legal instructions and parse-after-print on the printer's representable domain. Neither implies that printing can reconstruct comments, whitespace, or unresolved symbol names from a raw word.

\section{Test the Object Path Independently}

A parser change can affect accepted text while a format change can affect bytes without affecting the accepted mnemonic. A symbolic operand also creates metadata that must travel through the emitter to the linker; checking only the bytes with a zero placeholder will miss incorrect relocation kinds or symbols. Section~5.5 reports RISC-V tests building executables with generated assembler and linker components for use in simulator testing. The paper also describes syntax experiments on MIPS IV and AArch64. Those experiments are evidence about the prototype, not about every object-format feature or language combination; independent expected bytes and expected fixup records are still useful when tools generated from the same mistaken specification agree with one another.
